 ## Overview
-This configuration defines the hyperparameters, architectural modifications, and optimization settings required to train a Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) model using depth priors. It addresses the problem of geometric instability and texture-less region artifacts in standard NeRF training by integrating depth supervision. It differs from sibling methods like [CLIPNeRF](12073-clipnerf-training-configuration.md) by focusing on geometric consistency via depth metrics rather than semantic alignment, and from [Binary Radiance Fields](13622-binary-radiance-fields.md) by maintaining continuous density representations.
+This configuration defines the hyperparameters, architectural modifications, and optimization settings required to train a Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) model using depth priors. It addresses the problem of geometry regularization and convergence stability by integrating explicit depth supervision into the standard differentiable volume rendering pipeline. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by relying on a specific dropout-based regularization strategy and a fixed, empirically derived depth loss weight ($\lambda$) rather than adaptive uncertainty weighting or complex multi-scale encodings.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
